  [108/1150] "<think>\nAlright, so I need to figure out whether Joely should pass the message to Eden or Corey first to reach Reese the fastest. I remember that BFS is used for finding the shortest path in an unweighted graph, so I'll apply that here.\n\nFirst, I'll map out the connections. Let's see, Joely is friends with Corey and Joel. Corey is connected to Joely, Yitzchak, and Rechel. Joel is connected to Corey, Yitzchak, and Edan. Rechel is friends with Corey, Yitzchak, and Yitzchak is also friends with Montserrat. Yitzchak is connected to Aliza, who is friends with Eileen and Rees. Montserrat is friends with Edan and Yitzchak. Edan is friends with Joely, Yitzchak, and Rodney. Rodney is friends with Eileen and Shwan. Eileen is friends with Aliza and Rodney. Shwan is friends with Rees and Joely. Rees is friends with Joely and Caroline. Caroline is friends with Aliza and Oliver. Oliver is friends with Edan and Caroline. Aliza is friends with Eileen, Shwan, and Rees. Yitzchak is friends with Montserrat and Corey. Montserrat is friends with Yitzchak and Edan.\n\nSo, starting from Joely, the first step is to look at his direct friends: Corey and Joel. Then, from Corey, we can go to Yitzchak and Rechel. From Joel, we can go to Yitzchak and Edan. So the next layer would be Yitzchak and Rechel from Corey, and Edan from Joel. \n\nNow, looking at the target, Rees. Rees is connected to Joely and Caroline. So, if Joely sends the message to Corey, Corey can then pass it to Rees. Alternatively, if Joely sends to Joel, Joel can pass it to Caroline, who then can pass it to Rees. \n\nWait, but in BFS, the shortest path would be the one with the least steps. Let's see: Joely -> Corey -> Rees is two steps. Joely -> Joel -> Caroline -> Rees is three steps. So Corey is the better option because it's shorter.\n\nI think that's it. So Joely should pass to Corey first.\n</think>\n\neden -> corey -> reese\n\neden"  correct=eden
