hree steps. Therefore, either path is equally efficient. But the question asks which one to choose, so perhaps both are correct. But the user wants the shortest path, so both are same length. \n\nWait, maybe I should consider if there\'s a way through another node. For example, from Kait to Nevaeh to Russell to Colette? Let me check. Nevaeh is connected to Russell, and Russell is connected to Colette. So that\'s another path: Tiffany -> Kait -> Nevaeh -> Russell -> Colette. That\'s four steps, which is longer. So that\'s not better.\n\nSimilarly, from Katherine to Toby to Colette is three steps, which is better than this four-step path. So the shortest path is three steps for both options.\n\nSo both paths are equally efficient, but the question asks which one to choose. Since both are same length, either is correct. But the user wants the shortest path, so both are same. \n\nWait, maybe I should check if there\'s a way through another node. For example, from Kait to Kai to Colette is three steps. From Katherine to Toby to Colette is three steps. So both are same. \n\nTherefore, the shortest path is three steps, and both options are equally good. But the question asks which one to begin with, so either is correct. \n\nWait, but the user wants the answer to be either "christina" or "katherine". Since both are same, maybe either is acceptable. But the user might expect one. \n\nLooking back, the connections: Tiffany is connected to Kait, Faiga, and Katherine. So from Tiffany, the direct friends are Kait, Faiga, and Katherine. \n\nFrom Kait, the path is Kait -> Kai -> Colette. From Faiga, the path is Faiga -> Nevaeh -> Russell -> Colette, which is longer. From Katherine, the path is Katherine -> Toby -> Colette. \n\nSo the shortest paths are through Kait or Katherine, both three steps. \n\nTherefore, the answer is either path is equally efficient, but since the question asks which one to choose, both are correct. But the user wants the answer to be one word, so perhaps