  [250/1150] "<think>\nAlright, so I need to figure out whether Rebecca should pass the message to Tehila or Esteban to get to Morris the fastest. I remember that BFS is used for finding the shortest path in an unweighted graph, so I'll use that approach.\n\nFirst, I'll map out the connections. Rebecca is friends with Tehila, Freida, Esteban, and maybe others. From the data, Rebecca is friends with Tehila, Freida, and Esteban. So, starting from Rebecca, the first step is to look at her direct friends.\n\nTehila is connected to Rebecca and Freida, and also to Morris. So, if Rebecca sends the message to Tehila, the next step would be to Morris. That's two steps: Rebecca -> Tehila -> Morris.\n\nEsteban is connected to Rebecca, Freida, and also to Bruchy and others. But Esteban isn't directly connected to Morris. So, if Rebecca sends to Esteban, the message would go through someone else to get to Morris. Maybe Esteban -> Freida -> Rebeca -> ... Wait, but that's a loop. Alternatively, Esteban -> Bruchy -> ... Hmm, but Bruchy is connected to Aissatou and Egypt, and Aissatou is connected to Bruchy and Alessandra. Alessandra is connected to Eyad, and Eyad is connected to Egypt and Aissatou. Egypt is connected to Bruchy and Nicolle. Nicolle is connected to Egypt and Aissatou. So, from Esteban, the path would be Esteban -> Bruchy -> Egypt -> Nicolle -> ... but that's longer than the path through Tehila.\n\nWait, but maybe there's a shorter path through Freida. From Esteban, Freida is a friend. Freida is connected to Rebeca and Tehila. So, Esteban -> Freida -> Rebeca -> Tehila -> Morris. That's four steps, which is longer than the two-step path through Tehila.\n\nSo, the shortest path is Rebecca -> Tehila -> Morris. Therefore, Rebecca should pass the message to Tehila first.\n</think>\n\ntehila -> morris tehila"  correct=tehila
