  [334/1150] "<think>\nAlright, so I need to figure out whether Alessandra should pass the message to Ibrahim or Eyad to get to Tehila the fastest. I remember that BFS is used for finding the shortest path in an unweighted graph, so I'll use that approach.\n\nFirst, I'll map out the connections. Let's see, Alessandra is friends with Eyad and Aissatou. Eyad is friends with Egyd and Aissatou. Egyd is connected to Bruchy and Nicole. Nicole is friends with Egyd and Egypt. Egypt is connected to Bruchy and Aissatou. Bruchy is friends with Aissatou and Nicolle. Nicolle is friends with Egypt and Bruchy. \n\nWait, I'm getting a bit confused. Let me try to visualize this. Starting from Alessandra, the first step is to her friends: Eyad and Aissatou. From Eyad, he's connected to Egyd and Aissatou. But Aissatou is already visited. So Egyd is the next step. Egyd is connected to Bruchy and Nicole. \n\nFrom Bruchy, he's connected to Aissatou and Nicolle. Nicolle is connected to Egypt. Egypt is connected to Bruchy and Aissatou. So, the path from Alessandra to Egypt would be Alessandra -> Eyad -> Egyd -> Egypt. That's three steps.\n\nAlternatively, from Aissatou, she's connected to Bruchy. Bruchy is connected to Nicolle, who is connected to Egypt. So that's also three steps: Alessandra -> Aissatou -> Bruchy -> Egypt.\n\nWait, but I'm trying to get to Tehila. Let me check the connections for Tehila. Tehila is friends with Rebeccah and Morris. Rebeccah is friends with Freida and Esteban. Freida is friends with Rebeccah and Tehila. So, from Freida, she's connected to Rebeccah and Tehila. \n\nSo, starting from Alessandra, she can go to Eyad or Aissatou. If she goes to Eyad, she can reach Egyd, then to Egypt, then to Bruchy, then to Nicolle, then to Rebeccah, then to Freida, then to Tehila. That's a long path.\n\nAlternatively, if she goes to Aissatou, she can reach Bruchy, then to Nicolle, then to Egypt, then to Rebeccah, then to Freida, then to Tehila. Still the same number of steps.\n